{"agent":"fleet-load","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"The holder note survives a crashed holder, because it is only removed in the `finally` of a live\nprocess and the `with contextlib.suppress(OSError)` swallows any failure — yet the flock it\ndescribes is released by the kernel when the holder dies. `_holders` then reads notes for *all*\nindices `0..limit-1`, held or not, and `min(..., key=at)` deliberately prefers the *oldest* note,\ni.e. exactly the stale one. Concrete case with `ci.max_concurrent_suites = 2`: run A takes slot 0\nat 09:00 and is SIGKILLed at 09:05 (leaving `suites.0.holder`, slot now free), run B takes slot 1\nat 10:00, run C starts at 10:01 and times out after `suite_wait_s`. `_holders` returns A's note\n(older `at`) and the refused run reports \"blocked on <A> since 09:00:00\" even though A is gone and\nthe actual live blocker is B. The docstring's invariant (\"they are only read while the slot is\nheld\") is therefore false, and the message — the sole purpose of the note — is systematically\nwrong after any crash. Fix by clearing/ignoring notes for unheld slots (or by keying the message\noff which slots are currently locked rather than off file age).","digest":"921725a360954036","location":"ddflow/services/suitelock.py:46 (`_holders`) / :72 (`note.unlink()`)","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/suitelock.py:46 (`_holders`) / :72 (`note.unlink()`)"}],"chunks_total":5,"coverage":"4/5 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":16194,"diff_sha":"e0eb82c01eccbfc70cc6c7c74442ed1deb1e64db0841224ca83dc7bdae04104e","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":1830.7,"family":"deepseek","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"6688cd1730bb58cd","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-B26b5951f2b.rubber_duck.lan-d